{"t":1792802457,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"deaa54d2-f122-41c0-bd7f-0cd294a4902c","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-23T19:10:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 231910\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n310 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nA Frost Advisory has been issued for portions of northern\nMaryland late tonight into Saturday morning. The Small Craft\nAdvisory was cancelled, except for the middle Chesapeake and\nlower tidal Potomac, where it was extended until 2 AM.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in many areas\n  tonight, Saturday night, and Sunday night.\n\n- 2) Dry weather continues through next week along with \n  generally seasonable temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Frost or freezing temperatures are possible in\nmany areas tonight, Saturday night, and Sunday night.\n\nWith a large area of Canadian high pressure building overhead\nthrough the weekend, conditions will likely become favorable \nfor radiational cooling (light to calm winds, mainly clear skies\nand a cool background airmass). At the moment it is unclear\nwhich night will be the coldest, although Saturday night will\nhave a slight advantage given the position of the high.\n\nThe growing season for 2026 has ended along and west of the Blue\nRidge and Catoctin Mountains, but continues east of there. Any\nheadlines for frost/freeze conditions will only be issued where\nthe growing season remains active.\n\nFor tonight, temperatures look to fall into the mid to upper 30s\nfor many areas (slightly colder west of the Blue Ridge and\nslightly warmer in the urban areas and bayshore). The typically\ncolder spots of northern MD and the central VA piedmont could \nat least briefly come close to freezing. For the afternoon \npackage, a Frost Advisory was only issued from Frederick to \nCecil Counties. These areas will be closer to the surface high \nand have lower surface dew points. Farther to the south, there \nwill be a background easterly flow and perhaps some residual \nclouds, so the evening shift will watch trends to determine if \nthe advisory needs to be expanded.\n\nFor Saturday night, more widespread cold (possible frost/freeze)\nconditions are possible as high pressure settles more directly \noverhead. This would bring the frost/freeze threat for most \nareas outside the major urban centers and immediate shore of the\nChesapeake Bay. If the colder temperature guidance verifies, \nthen the growing season may end for more areas west I-95 come \nearly Sunday morning. The high will remain overhead through \nMonday morning, and the low level airmass doesn't change \nappreciably, so frost/freeze conditions will be possible again \nSunday night.\n\nDid not make appreciable changes to the previous min temperature\nforecasts, which are near or below the coldest guidance which \nperforms best in radiational cooling (MOS, NBM 10 percentile and\nCanadian Regional).\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues through next week along\nwith generally seasonable temperatures.\n\nCanadian high pressure will continue to prevail overhead over the \nnext several days as upper-level ridging builds in from the west. As \nthis ridging tracks east, a dry backdoor cold front will move \nthrough Tuesday and into Wednesday, with winds shifting \nnortheasterly in the wake of the front. With PWATs remaining dry \nthrough this period, no precipitation is anticipated as this front \nmoves through. \n\nAs this surface high pressure continues on, temperatures remain \nseasonable, with highs in the 60s to low-70s. Overnight lows could\ngradually increase somewhat throughout the week as this high \npressure persists, with skies remaining mostly clear throughout the \nweek. A few models hint at this high pressure breaking down towards \nthe end of next week as a weak trough begins to form over the Ohio \nRiver Valley next Thursday. Regardless, expect continued dry & \nseasonable conditions to continue at least through the next several \ndays as the current pattern remains largely unchanged. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected to prevail through early next week.\nThere are some hints that a slight uptick in low-level moisture\ncould lead to some patchy fog during the early morning hours\nthrough the weekend, though confidence in timing and extent of \nsub-VFR is low at this time.\n\nLight E to NE winds this afternoon will become light to calm \ntonight. A more E to SE flow is likely Saturday, with light to \ncalm winds again Saturday night. Wind direction may be a bit \nvariable, ranging from SE to NE as high pressure passes directly\noverhead Sunday. \n\nVFR conditions are anticipated to continue across terminals \nthrough Wednesday as conditions remain dry across the Mid-\nAtlantic. Light westerly winds Monday shift northerly on \nTuesday, then east to southeasterly on Wednesday in the wake of \na backdoor cold front.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nN to NE winds this afternoon will become more E to SE tonight\ninto Saturday. Winds have decreased below SCA levels this\nafternoon, but are forecast to increase again by late afternoon\nor early evening on the wider waters of the bay surrounding \nsouthern Maryland. Thus, northern portions of the advisory have \nbeen cancelled, but the lower Potomac to Tangier Sound have been\nextended through 2 AM. While winds may increase elsewhere, \nadvisory level gusts look to be brief in duration if they occur.\n\nHigh pressure will build overhead this weekend. Winds will vary\na bit from northeast to southeast, but at light speeds less than\n10 knots. Winds stay below SCA criteria Monday. A dry backdoor\ncold front passing through could briefly bring wind gusts near\nSCA criteria on Tuesday before diminishing again overnight. \nWesterly winds shift northerly Tuesday, then easterly Wednesday \nin the wake of the front.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nGenerally light but steady northeast to east winds are expected\nto keep water levels elevated through the upcoming weekend. \nWhen paired with high astronomical tides due to the upcoming \nfull moon on October 26, additional rounds of minor tidal \nflooding are possible. There has been a slight trend downward,\nso there is some decrease in confidence for which locations \nreach minor flooding in forthcoming high tides. Guidance does \nshow a more notable decline in water levels by Monday when flow \nfinally turns westerly.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Saturday for MDZ004>006-\n     008-507-508.\n     Coastal Flood Advisory until 7 PM EDT this evening for MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Frost Advisory from 1 AM to 9 AM EDT Saturday for ANZ530.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 2 AM EDT Saturday for ANZ534-537-\n     543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/DHOF/SRT\nAVIATION...ADS/DHOF/SRT\nMARINE...ADS/DHOF/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1792802419,"days":{"2026-10-23":{"hi":null,"lo":43,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Clear"},"2026-10-24":{"hi":64,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-25":{"hi":68,"lo":40,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-26":{"hi":70,"lo":50,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-27":{"hi":70,"lo":48,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-28":{"hi":66,"lo":50,"pop":1,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-29":{"hi":71,"lo":52,"pop":3,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":72,"lo":null,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Sunny"}}},"grid":{"t0":1792800000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,9.9,10.0,10.0,10.0,1